# Copy this file to repos.yaml and replace the example values with your local repositories.
# Relative paths are resolved from the directory containing repos.yaml.
# Add one additional "- name" entry under repositories for every repository you want to manage.
#
# Validation rules enforced by repo-status:
#   - The path must be a regular file; a directory, device, or pipe is rejected.
#   - The file must contain exactly one YAML document; a second document after "---" is rejected.
#   - Only the "repositories" key is allowed at the top level, and each entry allows only
#     "name" and "location"; unknown or misspelled fields are rejected.
#   - At least one repository must be defined; an empty or comments-only file is rejected.
#   - At most 1,000 repositories may be defined, and the file must be at most 1 MiB
#     (1,048,576 bytes). Reading is capped, so a larger or growing file is rejected.
#   - Names and locations must not be blank; surrounding whitespace is trimmed on load.
#   - Names must be unique, compared case-insensitively, so "api" and "API" conflict.
#   - Names must not contain a comma (it separates --repos selectors), a control character
#     such as a tab, newline, or escape, or a Unicode bidirectional formatting character.
#   - Locations are not checked on load, and only local folders are supported; a missing
#     folder or one that is not a Git repository is reported later by Git.
#
# Windows locations must be complete: "C:repos" (drive-relative) and "\repos" (drive-rooted)
# are rejected, as is an incomplete UNC path such as "\\server". Use a full path such as
# "C:\path\to\repository", a relative path, or a complete UNC path such as "\\server\share\repo".
# Alternate data stream paths such as "repos.yaml:stream" are rejected for this file, for every
# location, and for the -o output destination.
repositories:
  - name: my-local-repository
    location: C:\path\to\repository
